sues":0,"closed_issues":263,"state":"closed","created_at":"2018-11-27T04:11:27Z","updated_at":"2023-03-07T15:41:37Z","due_on":null,"closed_at":"2021-03-18T12:29:03Z"},"comments":9,"created_at":"2018-05-21T06:45:21Z","updated_at":"2020-08-02T03:18:31Z","closed_at":"2020-08-02T03:18:31Z","author_association":"COLLABORATOR","type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"**Migrated issue, originally created by Patrick Buxton**\n\nA type declared in a 3rd party dialect does not get resolved when you ask it to compile and results in a:\nUnsupportedCompilationError: Compiler <sqlalchemy.sql.compiler.GenericTypeCompiler object at 0x0000000005C48CC0> can't render element of type <class 'sqlalchemy_hana.types.TINYINT'>\n\n```\nfrom sqlalchemy import Table, MetaData, Column\nfrom sqlalchemy_hana.types import TINYINT\nmeta = MetaData()\nt = Table('test_table', meta, Column('Col1', TINYINT))\nstr(t.columns['Col1'].type)\n```\nThe problem stems from type_api _default_dialect where it's happy to lookup within the included dialects. Given that the 3rd party dialects are supposed to follow specific naming conventions, would it be possible to provide a lookup on that basis too?\n\n\n```\n    def _default_dialect(self, default):\n        if self.__class__.__module__.startswith(\"sqlalchemy.dialects\"):\n            tokens = self.__class__.__module__.split(\".\")[0:3]\n            mod = \".\".join(tokens)\n            return getattr(__import__(mod).dialects, tokens[-1]).dialect()\n        else:\n            return default.DefaultDialect()\n```\n\n","closed_by":{"login":"sqlalchemy-bot","id":36047385,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjM2MDQ3Mzg1","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/36047385?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/sqlalchemy-bot","html_url":"https://github.com/sqlalchemy-bot","follower